1
00:00:01,374 --> 00:00:09,687
Welcome to episode three of our U.S. states series. First, I clear the map so
we can study a fresh group.

2
00:00:10,351 --> 00:00:24,422
Today we connect the eight South Central states. We start with Kentucky and
Tennessee, move down to Alabama and Mississippi, then follow Louisiana,
Arkansas, Oklahoma and Texas westward.

3
00:00:25,094 --> 00:00:32,176
Let's begin with Kentucky above Tennessee, two long states stretching west
from the Appalachians.

4
00:00:35,309 --> 00:00:44,818
Kentucky stretches west from West Virginia, directly above Tennessee. Its
winding northern edge follows the Ohio River for much of the way.

5
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:54,524
The card's name origin is uncertain, but its cave fact is clear: Mammoth Cave
is the world's longest known cave system.

6
00:00:55,174 --> 00:01:03,023
Picture Kentucky as the upper half of a long east-west pair. Tennessee is the
lower half, and our next stop.

7
00:01:05,446 --> 00:01:14,618
Tennessee is the long, narrow state immediately south of Kentucky. It runs
from the Mississippi River in the west to the Appalachian mountains in the
east.

8
00:01:15,272 --> 00:01:24,990
Its name comes from Tanasi, a Cherokee town. The card notes that Tennessee
borders eight states, tying Missouri for the most.

9
00:01:25,644 --> 00:01:34,433
Keep Kentucky above Tennessee. Below Tennessee sit Mississippi, Alabama and
Georgia. We now turn south to Alabama.

10
00:01:35,116 --> 00:01:43,220
Now follow Alabama and Mississippi down toward the Gulf, then turn west along
the coast into Louisiana.

11
00:01:45,746 --> 00:01:53,885
Alabama sits west of Georgia and south of Tennessee. Its small coastal opening
reaches the Gulf beside Florida's panhandle.

12
00:01:54,549 --> 00:02:06,147
The card points north to Huntsville, nicknamed Rocket City. NASA's Marshall
Space Flight Center there helped develop the Saturn rockets used for Apollo.

13
00:02:06,809 --> 00:02:15,981
Keep this east-west sequence in mind: Georgia, Alabama, Mississippi. We move
one state farther west next.

14
00:02:18,288 --> 00:02:28,261
Mississippi lies immediately west of Alabama, below Tennessee. Its western
river border separates it from Arkansas and much of Louisiana.

15
00:02:28,921 --> 00:02:41,030
Its name comes from an Indigenous expression meaning great river. The
Mississippi Delta is closely associated with the origins and development of
the blues.

16
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:52,257
Here the musical Delta means a region in northwestern Mississippi, not simply
the river's mouth. Follow the river south toward Louisiana.

17
00:02:54,801 --> 00:03:05,842
Louisiana is the boot-shaped Gulf state west of Mississippi and south of
Arkansas. The Mississippi River reaches the coast through its southeastern
wetlands.

18
00:03:06,505 --> 00:03:16,060
The name honours Louis the Fourteenth of France. The Louisiana Purchase of
1803 covered a vast territory, much larger than today's state.

19
00:03:16,712 --> 00:03:27,033
Distinguish the modern state from that historic purchase. Keep Louisiana below
Arkansas, with Texas along its western side.

20
00:03:27,713 --> 00:03:34,958
Turn inland to Arkansas and Oklahoma. Louisiana is below Arkansas, while Texas
waits below Oklahoma.

21
00:03:37,256 --> 00:03:46,590
Arkansas lies directly north of Louisiana and west of the Mississippi River.
Oklahoma is its western neighbour; Missouri is above it.

22
00:03:47,247 --> 00:03:58,509
The card links its name to the Quapaw people through French spelling. At
Crater of Diamonds State Park, visitors can search for diamonds and keep their
finds.

23
00:03:59,162 --> 00:04:07,428
Remember Arkansas above Louisiana. Its final letter is silent, unlike Kansas,
which is a different state farther northwest.

24
00:04:09,921 --> 00:04:19,731
Oklahoma stretches west from Arkansas, directly above Texas. Notice its narrow
panhandle pointing west between Kansas and Texas.

25
00:04:20,388 --> 00:04:33,647
Its Choctaw-derived name means red people. The Sooner nickname recalls
settlers who entered land before the official start of the 1889 land run.

26
00:04:34,301 --> 00:04:45,818
Those land openings followed the dispossession of Native nations. For the map,
remember Oklahoma above Texas, with Arkansas on its eastern side.

27
00:04:46,510 --> 00:04:55,589
Finally, let's give Texas a wider frame, from its northern panhandle to the
Rio Grande and the Gulf Coast.

28
00:04:57,972 --> 00:05:08,549
Texas is the large state below Oklahoma and west of Louisiana. Its southern
river border, the Rio Grande, separates it from Mexico.

29
00:05:09,199 --> 00:05:19,346
The card traces its name to a Caddo word for friend or ally. Texas declared
independence in 1836 and joined the United States in 1845.

30
00:05:20,002 --> 00:05:30,103
Texas completes our eight South Central states. Its northern panhandle sits
below Oklahoma's, while its southeastern edge reaches the Gulf.

31
00:05:34,990 --> 00:05:46,763
Our first pair was Kentucky above Tennessee. Below them came Alabama and
Mississippi; then Louisiana below Arkansas, with Oklahoma above Texas farther
west.

32
00:05:47,417 --> 00:06:00,467
Those eight states combine the Census East and West South Central divisions.
Picture their linked pairs, with the Mississippi River and Gulf Coast helping
you keep your bearings.
